tion is when something is  coming into being it is being brought  into existence destruction or corruption  sometimes it's translated is the  opposite course something is being  destroyed or consumed could be another  way of thinking about that so those are  two very important types of motion or  change and then we have two other ones  that are closely connected with each  other  Alex ASIS which means increase  or growth you know amplification is how  it's actually translated in the rhetoric  where Aristotle use of the same term and  did munition or shrinkage or however  else you want to put it may osis right  this is a another way of changing and  these are you know connected with each  other they don't just follow each other  in sequence here they're connected with  each other logically as well  so once you actually have something that  has come into being then it can increase  or decrease it can be destroyed likewise  something that is is in the process of  destruction might undergo growth at the  same time think about you know when  cancer is growing within a person the  cancer is growing the person is being  destroyed then we have another one which  in Greek is literally becoming other a  loyal sister ed olives is the Greek term  for other or different and we we're  translating this alteration or you might  also translate a change in quality or  you know we could might think of these  as change in quantity this is change in  quality going from being red to being  green going from healthy to being sick  and vice versa and then we have what we  typically think of when we hear the word  motion which is change in place contet  open meta Bolle contet open means in  relation to place right at opals is a  place and meta bola means means change  as well so we have all six types here  and Aristotle wants to say that these  are all fundamentally different from  each other we can distinguish these and  single them out as real you know ways in  which things do change he wants to  stress as well as I put here that  alteration is not something reducible to  the other five motions he says in the  case of alterations somebody might  object  that a subject when altered is altered  by one of the other five motions so that  for example going from one color to  another or changing health to sickness  or goodness to badness or anything like  that is really more a matter of changing  the arrangement of the parts right or  it's an increase or a decrease or its  generation of some sort something new  coming into being or something being  destroyed Aristotle says two really  interesting things here the first is he  says look it's not really the case that  alteration can be reduced to these other  things he says for by all or at least  most affections right pave or  alterations are brought about in us that  have nothing in common with what those  other motions we've mentioned view says  that which is thereby affected doesn't  have to be increased or diminished or  undergo any such process so it follows  from this that alteration is something  distinct from those other types of  motion and he says weren't the same with  some other object the object would alter  it would straight away also be increased  or diminished or undergo some other  motion but that is not so of necessity  so because we can in fact you know  distinguish changing in color from  changing in place or diminution or  destruction or whatever they're not the  same thing  they could be happen to the second point  they could be happening at the same time  he goes on to say that there are some  things that increase and are not thereby  altered you know you've got this example  of the square and maybe says there could  be some things that do increase at the  same time as they're being altered but  that doesn't mean that the increase and  the alteration are the same thing or the  change in place I mean there are some  interesting questions that we could put  there like well you know  Aris